from lessons3 import L, les
les(id="usbdbg",t="USB host side, Type-C/PD, DFU, and USB debugging",tag="USB",
an="Debugging USB without a capture is like debugging a phone call by only hearing one side. usbmon/Wireshark lets you hear the host's side; a hardware analyzer (Beagle, Ellisys, Total Phase) is a wiretap on the cable that hears both sides plus the electrical layer.",
j=[("xHCI","The standard USB 3 host controller interface."),("usbmon","Linux kernel facility that captures USB traffic on the host (read in Wireshark)."),("Type-C / PD","USB-C connector and Power Delivery protocol over the CC wire: negotiates voltage/current, data roles and alternate modes."),("DRD / OTG","Dual-role: one port can be host or device."),("DFU","Device Firmware Upgrade class: standard protocol to download firmware over USB.")],
d="""<h4>Capture workflow (host PC)</h4><pre>sudo modprobe usbmon
lsusb -t                     # find bus number
sudo wireshark -i usbmon1    # or: sudo tshark -i usbmon1 -w cam.pcapng
# filters: usb.device_address==7 ; usb.setup.bRequest==6 ; usb.transfer_type==0x00 (isoc)</pre>
<p>Windows: USBPcap + Wireshark. For isoc video problems (dropped frames, short packets, FID errors), a hardware analyzer is worth it: usbmon sees URBs, not wire-level retries, chirps or link power states.</p>
<h4>Common failures and first moves</h4><table><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Check</th></tr><tr><td>Enumeration -71</td><td>PHY tuning, cable, VBUS, eye diagram, D+ pull-up timing</td></tr><tr><td>Works on Linux, not Windows</td><td>wTotalLength, string descriptors, BOS/MS OS descriptors, bcdDevice caching</td></tr><tr><td>Video stutters on some laptops</td><td>isoc bandwidth contention with other devices on same xHCI, alt setting too large, LPM (U1/U2) exit latency</td></tr><tr><td>Device drops after suspend</td><td>remote wakeup, VBUS session, PM runtime in UDC driver</td></tr><tr><td>Charging but no data on USB-C</td><td>CC orientation mux, role (DRD) state, PD contract, Type-C HAL</td></tr></table>
<h4>Type-C and PD</h4><p>CC pins detect attach and orientation; the PD controller (often TI TPS65xx, or SoC-integrated) negotiates power and data role and DisplayPort alt mode. Linux: <code>typec</code> class (<code>/sys/class/typec</code>), <code>tcpm</code> for TCPCI chips, <code>ucsi</code> for firmware-managed controllers. Data role swap = UDC unbind ↔ xHCI start in the DRD driver.</p>
<h4>DFU and firmware delivery</h4><p>DFU 1.1: DFU_DNLOAD blocks → GET_STATUS polling → manifest → reset. Many products use vendor HID or bulk protocols instead; whichever, enforce: signed images, version/anti-rollback, A/B or fallback, power-loss safety, and resumable transfers (Lab 09). On Linux hosts test with <code>dfu-util -l</code>, <code>dfu-util -D fw.bin</code>.</p>""",
anim=("seq","USB-C attach and role decision",["Laptop (DFP)","CC / PD controller","Device SoC (DRD)"],[(0,1,"Rp on CC: I am a source/host"),(1,1,"detect orientation (CC1/CC2), set mux"),(1,0,"PD: request 20V/3A contract"),(1,2,"role = device (UFP)"),(2,2,"DRD switches to gadget, start UDC"),(2,0,"D+ pull-up → enumeration"),(1,0,"optional: DP alt mode enter")]),
q=[("usbmon shows URBs complete fine but the analyzer shows many retries. Why does usbmon not show them?","usbmon sits at the URB level in the host driver; link-level retries and PHY events happen below it."),("Why bump bcdDevice when descriptors change?","Windows caches device configuration by VID/PID/bcdDevice; a new value forces fresh enumeration handling.")],lab=None)

les(id="android",t="Android below the framework: AOSP, Treble, HALs, init, SELinux, GKI",tag="Android",
an="Treble is like a building with a fixed electrical panel. Google can replace the apartment (Android framework) without rewiring the walls (vendor HALs and drivers), as long as both sides use the standard plugs (versioned HAL interfaces).",
j=[("AOSP","Android Open Source Project: the full Android source."),("Treble","Split between framework (system) and vendor implementations with stable interfaces (VINTF)."),("AIDL HAL","Hardware interfaces defined in AIDL (HIDL is the older format)."),("init.rc","Android init's script language: services, triggers, properties."),("SELinux","Mandatory access control; every process and file has a label and every access must be allowed."),("GKI","Generic Kernel Image: Google's common kernel; vendor code goes into loadable modules.")],
d="""<h4>Partitions</h4><p><code>boot</code> (GKI kernel + generic ramdisk), <code>init_boot</code>, <code>vendor_boot</code> (vendor ramdisk, DTB, vendor modules for first stage), <code>system</code>, <code>system_ext</code>, <code>product</code>, <code>vendor</code>, <code>odm</code>, <code>vendor_dlkm</code> (vendor kernel modules), <code>vbmeta</code>. With A/B, each has _a/_b.</p>
<h4>Boot</h4><p>Bootloader → kernel → first-stage init (mounts, loads vendor_boot modules) → SELinux policy load → second-stage init parses <code>/system/etc/init</code>, <code>/vendor/etc/init/*.rc</code> → servicemanager, hwservicemanager → HAL services (class hal) → zygote → system_server.</p>
<h4>Writing a vendor HAL (Lab 11 files)</h4><ul><li>Define <code>IUsbCamControl.aidl</code> with <code>@VintfStability</code>; Soong <code>aidl_interface</code> with ndk backend; freeze versions (<code>m vendor.poly.usbcam-update-api</code>, <code>-freeze-api</code>).</li><li>Implement the service in C++ (NDK backend, <code>AServiceManager_addService</code>), add to VINTF manifest fragment.</li><li>init .rc fragment to start it, file_contexts + .te policy so it can run and touch configfs.</li></ul>
<h4>SELinux workflow</h4><p><code>adb logcat | grep avc</code> or <code>dmesg | grep avc</code> → understand each denial → add the narrowest rule → never ship permissive. <code>audit2allow</code> is a starting point, not an answer; neverallow rules (CTS/VTS) will reject broad grants.</p>
<h4>GKI rules</h4><p>No vendor changes to the core kernel image; your drivers become modules using the stable KMI symbol list. If you need a new exported symbol, it must be requested upstream to Android Common Kernel. Plan for this when porting vendor BSP drivers.</p>
<h4>Daily commands</h4><pre>adb shell getprop | grep usb ; adb shell dumpsys usb ; adb shell lshal / service list
adb shell cat /proc/cmdline ; adb shell dmesg -w ; adb logcat -b all
m -j vendorimage ; fastboot flash vendor_a vendor.img ; adb remount (userdebug)</pre>""",
anim=("flow","Android boot path",["Bootloader (ABL) + AVB","Kernel (GKI) + vendor_boot modules","First-stage init: mount, load modules","SELinux policy loaded","Second-stage init: *.rc","servicemanager + vendor HALs","zygote → system_server → apps"],["verifies vbmeta","generic kernel, vendor .ko","/vendor, /system mounted","enforcing","on early-init / init / boot triggers","your IUsbCamControl starts","framework calls your HAL"]),
q=[("Your HAL starts but can't write configfs. Log shows 'avc: denied { write }'. Fix?","Add a narrow allow for the HAL domain on configfs files/dirs in vendor sepolicy; don't make the domain permissive."),("Why can't you just patch the GKI kernel for a vendor driver?","GKI must stay generic; vendor code must be modules against the stable KMI, upstreamed to ACK if core changes are needed.")],lab="11-android-hal-init-sepolicy")

les(id="rtos",t="MCU and RTOS firmware, TinyUSB, bare-metal",tag="MCU",
an="An RTOS is an ER triage nurse: every tick, the most urgent ready patient is seen. Priority inversion is when the heart-attack patient waits because a sprained-ankle patient holds the only stretcher and the flu patient keeps getting seen instead.",
j=[("RTOS","Real-time OS (FreeRTOS, Zephyr, ThreadX): tasks, priorities, deterministic scheduling."),("Priority inheritance","Temporarily raising a low task holding a mutex to the waiter's priority."),("TinyUSB","Open-source USB device/host stack for microcontrollers."),("Watchdog","A timer that resets the chip unless software 'kicks' it regularly."),("Linker script","Says where code/data go in flash and RAM.")],
d="""<h4>Where MCUs appear in conferencing gear</h4><p>Mic pods, touch controllers, PD controllers, camera motor/PTZ, power sequencing, LED rings. Many have their own USB stack (TinyUSB, vendor stacks) and their own firmware update path driven by the main SoC.</p>
<h4>RTOS essentials</h4><ul><li>ISR → queue/semaphore → task; never block in ISRs (use <code>xQueueSendFromISR</code> and yield).</li><li>Mutex (with inheritance) for shared resources; binary semaphore for signaling. Lab 10 shows inversion: HIGH finishes at tick 11 without inheritance, tick 5 with it.</li><li>Stack sizing (<code>uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark</code>), heap fragmentation, watchdog per task health.</li></ul>
<h4>TinyUSB</h4><p>Callbacks: <code>tud_descriptor_device_cb</code>, <code>tud_descriptor_configuration_cb</code>, class callbacks (<code>tud_hid_set_report_cb</code>, <code>tud_audio_*</code>), and call <code>tud_task()</code> from a task/main loop; the DCD port runs in the USB ISR. TinyUSB examples build for many boards; to run without hardware, build the host-side unit tests (Ceedling) or use its examples under a simulator. (Not run on our box.)</p>
<h4>Bare-metal bring-up</h4><p>Reset vector → copy .data, zero .bss → clocks → <code>main</code>. Debug with SWD/JTAG (OpenOCD + gdb), check hardfault registers (CFSR, HFSR, MMFAR, BFAR on Cortex-M) to find the faulting address.</p>""",
anim=("seq","Priority inversion vs inheritance",["LOW (holds mutex)","MED","HIGH (USB task)"],[(0,0,"LOW takes mutex"),(2,0,"HIGH wants mutex → blocks"),(1,1,"no inheritance: MED preempts LOW, runs long"),(2,2,"HIGH stuck (inversion)"),(0,0,"with inheritance: LOW boosted to HIGH"),(0,2,"LOW releases mutex"),(2,2,"HIGH runs immediately")]),
q=[("What famous mission had a priority inversion bug?","Mars Pathfinder (1997): fixed remotely by enabling priority inheritance on a VxWorks mutex."),("Cortex-M hard fault: which registers tell you the bad address?","CFSR flags plus MMFAR (memory manage) or BFAR (bus fault) when their valid bits are set; stacked PC tells the instruction.")],lab="10-rtos-scheduler-sim")

les(id="debug",t="Debugging toolbox: JTAG, gdb, ftrace, perf, oops decoding",tag="Debug",
an="Debugging is detective work. dmesg is the witness statement, ftrace is the CCTV timeline, perf is the traffic counter, JTAG is freezing time and walking around the crime scene, and an oops is the chalk outline telling you exactly where it happened.",
j=[("Oops","A kernel error report with registers and call trace; may or may not panic."),("ftrace","Kernel's built-in tracer (/sys/kernel/tracing)."),("perf","Sampling profiler for CPU hotspots and events."),("JTAG / SWD","Hardware debug ports to halt CPUs and read memory/registers."),("addr2line / faddr2line","Turn a function+offset into a source line.")],
d="""<h4>Decode the sample oops (Lab 12)</h4><ol><li><b>Type</b>: NULL pointer dereference at virtual address 0x18 → a field at offset 0x18 of a NULL struct pointer.</li><li><b>Where</b>: <code>pc : polybridge_hpd_work+0x3c/0x120 [polybridge]</code>, in a workqueue (<code>Workqueue: events polybridge_hpd_work</code>).</li><li><b>Line</b>: <code>./scripts/faddr2line polybridge.ko polybridge_hpd_work+0x3c</code> (needs the .ko with debug info), or <code>gdb polybridge.ko -ex 'list *(polybridge_hpd_work+0x3c)'</code>.</li><li><b>Theory</b>: the work ran after remove()/before probe finished set a pointer (e.g. <code>bridge-&gt;connector</code>). Fix by cancel_work_sync in remove and only scheduling after init completes.</li><li><b>Tainted: O</b> means out-of-tree module loaded: expected for vendor drivers, but note it in bug reports.</li></ol>
<h4>ftrace recipes</h4><pre>cd /sys/kernel/tracing
echo function_graph > current_tracer; echo 'dwc3_*' > set_ftrace_filter; echo 1 > tracing_on
echo 1 > events/irq/enable ; echo 1 > events/v4l2/enable ; cat trace_pipe
trace-cmd record -e dwc3 -e xhci-hcd -p function_graph -g uvc_v4l2_qbuf</pre>
<h4>perf</h4><p><code>perf top</code>, <code>perf record -g -a sleep 10; perf report</code> for CPU hogs (e.g. memcpy in a UVC path → use dma-buf). <code>perf sched latency</code> for scheduling delays in audio threads.</p>
<h4>Other weapons</h4><ul><li><code>dynamic_debug</code>: <code>echo 'module dwc3 +p' &gt; /sys/kernel/debug/dynamic_debug/control</code>.</li><li>KASAN/KCSAN/lockdep debug builds; <code>kmemleak</code>.</li><li>pstore/ramoops to keep logs across reboots; kdump on larger systems.</li><li>JTAG (Lauterbach TRACE32, OpenOCD) for hangs before console and for multi-core lockups.</li><li>Android: <code>bugreport</code>, tombstones (<code>/data/tombstones</code>) for native crashes, <code>simpleperf</code>, Perfetto traces.</li></ul>""",
anim=("flow","Reading an oops",["'Unable to handle NULL pointer at 0x18'","ESR / Mem abort info","Modules linked in / Tainted","pc: function+offset [module]","Call trace (workqueue)","faddr2line → source line","Root cause + fix + regression test"],["what: NULL->field@0x18","data abort, EL1","which out-of-tree code was loaded","where exactly","how we got there","exact line","cancel_work_sync in remove()"]),
q=[("NULL deref 'at virtual address 0x18' means?","Code read a member at offset 0x18 through a NULL struct pointer."),("Which tool shows every DWC3 ep0 request and TRB completion?","The dwc3 tracepoints in ftrace (events/dwc3).")],lab="12-oops-decoding")

les(id="pm",t="Power management, networking, Wi-Fi/BT and security",tag="Platform",
an="Power management is turning off lights in empty rooms without plunging a busy room into darkness. Security is locks on every door, including the side door (debug ports) the builders used.",
j=[("Runtime PM","Per-device power down when idle (pm_runtime_get/put)."),("System suspend","Whole-system sleep (suspend-to-RAM)."),("USB LPM / U1/U2","USB link power states; exit latency can glitch isoc streams."),("TEE","Trusted Execution Environment for keys and secure services."),("cfg80211/mac80211","Linux Wi-Fi frameworks; BlueZ (Linux) / Fluoride-Gabeldorsche (Android) for Bluetooth.")],
d="""<h4>Power</h4><ul><li>Implement <code>runtime_suspend/resume</code> and system <code>suspend/resume</code> in drivers; balance <code>pm_runtime_get_sync/put</code> (unbalanced counts keep devices awake or off).</li><li>Debug: <code>/sys/kernel/debug/wakeup_sources</code>, <code>/sys/power/pm_test</code>, <code>no_console_suspend</code>, <code>cat /sys/devices/.../power/runtime_status</code>.</li><li>USB: selective suspend, remote wakeup for button press, LPM disable quirks when streams glitch.</li></ul>
<h4>Networking / Wi-Fi / BT</h4><p>Conferencing devices are network appliances: know <code>ip</code>, <code>ethtool</code>, <code>tcpdump</code>, QoS/DSCP marking for RTP, VLANs, 802.1X for enterprise networks, <code>iw</code>/<code>wpa_supplicant</code>, Wi-Fi firmware load paths (<code>/lib/firmware</code>), BT HCI over UART/USB (<code>btmon</code> to capture). BT audio (HFP/A2DP/LE Audio) for headsets paired to room systems.</p>
<h4>Security</h4><ul><li>Lock JTAG/UART in production (fuses, auth debug), disable adb in user builds.</li><li>Parse every USB/HDMI/network input as hostile: descriptor lengths, EDID blocks, HID reports (fuzz them: syzkaller has USB fuzzing via raw-gadget).</li><li>Secrets in TEE/keystore, not files. Signed updates, verified boot, dm-verity, SELinux enforcing.</li><li>SBOM + CVE tracking for kernel and vendor BSP; plan monthly security patch merges.</li></ul>""",
anim=("flow","Runtime PM of a camera sensor",["App opens /dev/video0","pm_runtime_get_sync()","resume: regulators on, clocks, reset release","stream","app closes","pm_runtime_put_autosuspend()","suspend after delay: clocks/regulators off"],["usage count 1","driver callback","power sequence from datasheet","usage>0 keeps it on","usage count 0","autosuspend timer","saves power, avoids flapping"]),
q=[("Video stutters only when nothing else is plugged into the laptop. Hypothesis?","USB link power management (U1/U2/LPM) kicking in between transfers; exit latency hurts isoc. Test with LPM disabled."),("Why fuzz USB descriptors and EDID parsing?","They come from untrusted external devices; malformed input is a classic memory-corruption attack path.")],lab=None)

les(id="ota",t="OTA/DFU updates, testing and CI for firmware",tag="Quality",
an="Updating firmware in the field is replacing an airplane engine mid-flight: you keep one good engine running (slot A), install the new one (slot B), test it, and if it coughs you switch back automatically.",
j=[("A/B update","Two copies of partitions; update the inactive one, switch on reboot, roll back if boot fails."),("HIL","Hardware-in-the-loop: automated tests on real boards in a rack."),("VTS/CTS","Android Vendor/Compatibility Test Suites."),("KUnit / kselftest","Kernel unit tests and self tests."),("Bisect","Binary search through commits to find which one broke something.")],
d="""<h4>Update design checklist (Lab 09 demonstrates)</h4><ul><li>Verify integrity (CRC/hash) and authenticity (signature) before switching.</li><li>Anti-rollback version check.</li><li>Boot counter + watchdog → automatic fallback; mark 'successful' only after health checks (Android: <code>update_engine</code> + <code>bootctrl</code> HAL markBootSuccessful).</li><li>Accessory firmware (MCU, PD controller, camera module) updated from the main SoC with the same guarantees, and version compatibility matrix.</li><li>Power-loss testing: cut power at every stage in HIL.</li></ul>
<h4>Testing strategy (a senior deliverable)</h4><ol><li><b>Unit</b>: host-built C/C++ logic with sanitizers (like Labs 04/05/08/09/10). KUnit for kernel helpers.</li><li><b>Virtual</b>: QEMU runs (our Lab 00 harness), dummy_hcd gadget tests, vivid camera tests, v4l2-compliance.</li><li><b>HIL rack</b>: real devices + USB switch hubs (e.g. programmable), HDMI generators/analyzers, power relays; nightly enumerate-1000-times tests on Windows/macOS/Linux hosts.</li><li><b>Certification</b>: Teams/Zoom device certification, USB-IF compliance (USB3CV, Chapter 9 tests), Android CTS/VTS for GMS builds.</li><li><b>Field telemetry</b>: crash/oops collection, enumeration failures, frame-drop counters.</li></ol>
<h4>CI</h4><p>Every merge: build all products, run unit + QEMU tests, static analysis, checkpatch; nightly: HIL. Keep flaky-test dashboards; flakes hide real regressions. Use <code>git bisect run</code> with an automated test to find regressions fast.</p>""",
anim=("seq","A/B update with automatic rollback",["Update agent","Bootloader","Slot A (good)","Slot B (new)"],[(0,3,"write image to inactive B"),(0,3,"verify hash + signature + version"),(0,1,"set B active, tries=3"),(1,3,"boot B"),(3,1,"no markBootSuccessful (crash)"),(1,3,"retry ... tries=0"),(1,2,"fallback to A"),(2,0,"report failure telemetry")]),
q=[("When should a device mark the new slot successful?","Only after post-boot health checks pass (key services, USB enumerates, camera streams), not just when the kernel boots."),("Why run enumerate-1000-times tests on several host OSes?","Enumeration timing and descriptor handling bugs are intermittent and host-specific.")],lab="09-dfu-ab-image")

les(id="senior",t="Growing to senior/staff in this team",tag="Career",
an="A senior engineer fixes the hard bug. A staff engineer makes sure the whole team stops creating that class of bug: by changing the design, the tools, or the process.",
j=[("Design doc","A written proposal with context, options, trade-offs, decision, rollout and test plan."),("Blast radius","How many products/users a change can affect."),("Lean/agile","Small batches, fast feedback, remove waste (waiting, rework)."),("Brag doc","Your own running log of impact with evidence.")],
d="""<h4>The postings' senior verbs, turned into habits</h4><table><tr><th>Posting says</th><th>Do this</th></tr><tr><td>Lead secure, scalable, high-performance C/C++</td><td>Own one subsystem end to end (e.g. USB gadget/UVC path). Publish its architecture page, performance budget and threat model.</td></tr><tr><td>Define architecture, standards, best practices</td><td>Write a 'USB descriptor and composite rules' standard and a driver review checklist; get it adopted.</td></tr><tr><td>Troubleshoot complex system-level issues</td><td>Be the person who brings the analyzer trace and the root-cause write-up; keep a public debugging playbook.</td></tr><tr><td>Planning, docs, testing strategy</td><td>Propose the test pyramid for your area (unit → QEMU → HIL); land CI jobs.</td></tr><tr><td>Mentor</td><td>Run a monthly 'deep dive' session; pair on bugs; review with teaching comments.</td></tr><tr><td>Agile/lean</td><td>Measure something (build time, flake rate, time-to-root-cause) and improve it.</td></tr></table>
<h4>Showing impact</h4><ul><li>Quantify: '1080p30 UVC dropped frames from 2.1% to 0.05% on Windows hosts', 'enumeration failures in field telemetry down 80%', 'BSP rebase time from 3 weeks to 4 days'.</li><li>Write it down weekly in a brag doc; share short updates with your manager.</li><li>Vendor relations: clear, reproducible tickets to Qualcomm/NXP/Ambarella/Lontium; track SLAs; that visibility is staff-level work.</li><li>Upstream when allowed (with legal/manager approval): a few kernel patches with your name build external credibility.</li></ul>""",
anim=("flow","From fixing to multiplying",["Fix the bug","Write the root-cause note","Add a regression test","Add a checklist/CI rule","Teach it (deep dive)","Change the design so the class of bug can't happen"],["senior baseline","shared knowledge","never again here","never again anywhere","team capability","staff impact"]),
q=[("What turns a bug fix into staff-level impact?","Preventing the whole class of bug for the team: design change, tooling/CI rule, standard, and teaching."),("Give one measurable USB-area metric you could own.","Enumeration success rate across host matrix, frame-drop rate, time-to-first-frame, or update failure rate.")],lab=None)
